ideas.problem_and_gap:
1) The reported numbers. scMINER reports activity-based clustering ARI of 0.87 on PBMC14K (SCENIC 0.69) and 0.998 on a Treg tissue dataset (https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-59620-6).

2) The scoring code (`scMINER R/network_analysis.R`, `main`, 2026-09-16). `getActivity_inBatch`:
- subsets cells by `group_name` (documented as "usually the column of cell types or clusters");
- scores each group's cells only with that group's network;
- merges the per-group matrices with `merge(..., all = TRUE)`;
- fills every NA with the global matrix minimum.

So a driver missing from one group's network becomes a constant for exactly that group's cells. That writes a group indicator into the features, and target sets also differ by group.

3) The benchmark scripts (`scMINER_analysis activity_estimation/PBMC/plot_results_PBMC.Rmd` and `/Tregs/plot_results_Tregs.Rmd`). Both:
- load `scMINER_per_cell_type_activity_mean_netbid.Rdata`;
- apply the same min fill (`ac_tf_sing[is.na(ac_tf_sing)] = min(...)`);
- cluster with MICA;
- compute ARI against `pData(eset)$true_label` (PBMC) or tissue labels (Tregs).

The file name implies networks built per cell type. The paper does not say whether those are the same labels used for ARI (UNVERIFIED). If they are, the 0.87 and 0.998 are partly label leakage, and the comparison with SCENIC, whose regulons come from all cells pooled, is not like for like.

4) Even with MICA clusters as the groups, cluster, then per-cluster networks, then activity, then recluster is a self-reinforcing loop.

5) Related pipelines. PISCES (Vlahos et al., bioRxiv 2021) also builds per-cluster ARACNe networks, but scores cells with metaVIPER against all networks, which is a partial mitigation. I found no study that quantifies leakage in either pipeline. My Europe PMC check (2026-09-16) matched only on metadata, so this is weak evidence.
